>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This Entity-Relationship \(ER\) diagram meticulously outlines the database schema for the e-commerce backend. At its core, the USER table manages customer, seller, and admin accounts, linking to their respective PRODUCTs \(for sellers\) and ORDER_TABLEs \(for customers\). Product information is detailed in the PRODUCT table, which maintains a hierarchical relationship with CATEGORY for organization and connects to PRODUCT_IMAGE for multiple visuals. The purchasing BLoC revolves around the ORDER_TABLE, which aggregates ORDER_ITEMs \(detailing each product purchased at a specific price\), and is associated with PAYMENT records and potentially COUPONs for discounts. User engagement features like REVIEWs for products, a WISHLIST_ITEM system, and NOTIFICATIONs are directly linked to users and products. A CART and CART_ITEM system facilitates the shopping BLoC before an order is placed. Furthermore, a content-driven feature is supported by FEEL \(short video content\) and FEEL_LIKE tables, allowing sellers to showcase products and users to interact. Relationships are primarily one-to-many \(e.g., a user can have multiple orders\) or one-to-one \(e.g., a user has one cart\), with appropriate foreign keys, unique constraints, and data types defined to ensure data integrity and facilitate efficient querying. Key attributes like timestamps, soft delete flags \(for PRODUCT\), and versioning \(for PRODUCT\) enhance data management and auditability.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
